    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-177740",
      "sequence_position": 11,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253347/assets/6e876c632890560c5e968651f1b80828b3813ec915422d37b902d9d0c5c4b944.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P∧Q?$",
      "worked_solution": "The truth set of $P(x,y)∧Q(x,y)$ represents the *intersection* of the truth sets of $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P∧Q}={T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x,y)∧Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to both ${T}_{P}$ and ${T}_{Q},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253347/assets/433c96a8564075054a36ac7586da538791813ff42111543c5cfe012157e9f6be.png)\n\n  \n\n**Note:** The point where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* line does *not* belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$, since this point is *not* in  ${T}_{Q}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
